 ## Background
-The GGX microfacet model was introduced by Walter et al. in "Microfacet Models for Refraction through Rough Surfaces" (2007) [S1, unofficial]. It replaced the Beckmann distribution with the GGX (Generalized Trowbridge-Reiss) distribution, which provides a better fit for the long tails of specular highlights observed in real-world materials. The model is widely used in modern real-time and offline renderers, including Unreal Engine and Filament [S1, unofficial][S3].
+The GGX microfacet model was introduced by Walter et al. in "Microfacet Models for Refraction through Rough Surfaces" [S1, unofficial]. It uses the Generalized Trowbridge-Reiss (GGX) distribution to approximate surface reflection properties, providing a better fit for the long tails of specular highlights observed in real-world materials compared to earlier distributions. It is referenced in implementations for realistic metal and plastic surfaces [S3].
 
